Averaging over a target set $T$ gives $\overline\psi_T+D_T(S)$ with $D_T(S)=|T|^{-1}F_T(S)$, the design term that S6 minimises. The term depends on the run set only through $\sum_{j\in S}\psi_j^{-1}v_jv_j^{\top}$ and is non-increasing as benchmarks are added. Let $F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)=\overline\psi_T+D_T(S)$ be the prediction of the mean squared error for the fitted parameters, and let $\mathrm{MSE}_T(S)$ and $\mathrm{MSE}_T(C)$ be the measured error with run set $S$ and with all of the pool revealed. The measured error then splits exactly into four terms,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{MSE}_T(S)=\underbrace{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{noise}}+\underbrace{\mathrm{MSE}_T(C)-F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{floor misfit}}+\underbrace{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)-F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)}_{\text{design}}+\underbrace{\mathrm{rest}(S)}_{\text{estimation and misspecification}},
\label{eq:decomp}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{rest}(S)$ is whatever is left. With the parameters estimated from $M$ models rather than known, the extra error from estimating $V$ and $\psi$ is of order $1/M$ per model; the remainder in (\ref{eq:decomp}) holds that error and whatever else the other three terms do not account for; the data do not separate the two, and Section~\ref{sec:experiments} gives the size of the estimation term from data simulated from the model.

